I get information about my students in a roster spreadsheet, and the full name of each student appears in a single column as "LName, Fname OtherNames".  Eveery semester, I want to tease this apart into a column for the Fname and a column for the Lname.  For example, I would want to use "Miller, Jason Earl" to put "Jason" in a Fname column and "Miller" in a Lname column.  (I'd ignore the middle name.)  There are spreadsheet commands for to do this, and each semester I need to recreate those from scratch.  This ends now.

I'm writing this down for future reference, so I don't need to puzzle through the same syntax every semester.

To get "Jason" from the A1 cell that contains "Miller, Jason Earl" I use the command LEFT(A1,FIND(",",A1)−1).

To get "Miller" from the A1 cell that contains "Miller, Jason Earl" I use the command MID(B2,FIND(",",B2)+1,IFERROR(FIND(" ",RIGHT(B2,LEN(B2)−FIND(",",B2))),LEN(B2))).  Note that this command adapts to whether or not the Registrar puts a middle name in the A cell or not.  By using the IFERROR() command, I can use the location of a 'space' in my calculation if there is a space to the right of the comma; if there is no space and the FIND() command pukes, IFERROR() substitutes the output of the LEN() command.

I'm sure I've done this different ways in past semesters.  This is the solution that comes to me today.  My future self thanks me.